 "structure": "Maine Learning Results: Social Studies (2019 revised). Front matter (Introduction, Guiding Principles, Skills in Social Studies, Key Ideas, enduring themes, eras table, Maine statutes) then 16 tables, four strands (Civics & Government, Personal Finance & Economics, Geography, History) x four grade bands (K-2, 3-5, 6-8, 9-Diploma). Each table: rows Strand / Standard (statement), band name (Childhood, Early Adolescence, Adolescence), a header row of grade columns (Kindergarten|Grade 1|Grade 2 or Grade 3|Grade 4|Grade 5, or a single \"Grades 6-8\" / \"Grades 9-Diploma\"), then Performance Expectations cells. In K-5 tables each grade column cell holds numbered performance expectations (e.g. Civics & Government 1..n; Personal Finance / Economics / Global Connections; Geography 1-2; History 1-3); an asterisk (*) marks Guiding Principles. In 6-8 and 9-Diploma tables the strand's performance expectations are split into stems (e.g. \"Civics & Government 1: Students understand ... by:\") followed by two cells: F = Foundational (F1..) and D = Developmental (D1..) levels. 9-Diploma = grades 9-12. End: Definitions. No separate examples or teacher notes.",
